result.verdict:
Incremental. It is worth one day of kill tests (Step 0a + 0b, about 3 GPU-h), but not worth building the Dec 4 abstract around until D beats both matched-dose self-imitation and floor annealing at the 90% mark.

Every mechanism in it is already published:
- self-imitation of own top-advantage actions with a self-fading gate (SIL 2018, TSIL 2026, ESIL 2020);
- a separate consolidation phase on own successes in block stacking (PAIR 2022);
- top-q advantage regression as an EM step, with the mean allowed to move fast at large sigma (V-MPO);
- the stochastic-train / deterministic-deploy trade-off (Montenegro ICML 2024, C-PG 2025);
- 'regress onto own successes = EM' and 'compress pass@k into pass@1' on the LLM-RL side (ReST-EM, OREAL, Adaptive Guidance, Sharpening, BOND).

I found no paper that gates the pull on the measured gap between noisy and deterministic success, so that gate is the only new element, and it is thin.

On today's data, the most likely outcome is that E (anneal or remove the floor after ignition) or B (EMA weights) captures most of the effect. Any gain from the gate at 90% is probably small, because the deterministic curve already catches up near 90%. The PushRetreat dip is more simply explained by a from-scratch critic with the KL limit turned off (target_kl=1e9) than by the mean being too slow.

If the idea survives, narrow the claim to: 'exploration floors leave a deploy gap; gating self-imitation on the measured gap closes it without slowing runs that do not need it'. Lead with an a2r confirmation of O-219, which matters because a2r's student is distilled from the teacher mean. Choose either the EM claim or the Cauchy pull, not both, and drop the >=1.3x figure until it is measured. If Step 0a shows that O-219 is not the noise gap, or Step 0b shows that one offline pull does not move deterministic success, drop the idea.
